[[:db/retract 17592186122642 :answer-field/choices 17592186122643] [:db/retract 17592186122642 :answer-field/choices 17592186122644] [:db/retract 17592186122642 :answer-field/choices 17592186122645] [:db/retract 17592186122642 :answer-field/choices 17592186122646] [:db/retract 17592186122642 :answer-field/choices 17592186122647] {:db/id 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-55146915-2d47-5b59-9386-37303589ba19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125007 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1,\\pi )=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi )=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1,\\pi ) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122642 :answer-field/correct "answer-f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer-field/choices ["answer-783e5bf5-dd91-511c-81fe-eca19c21cfc0" "answer-f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" "answer-bb0f50fc-3f9e-56b0-874f-3d40ee14ed33" "answer-df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" "answer-3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783e5bf5-dd91-511c-81fe-eca19c21cfc0" :answer/id #uuid "783e5bf5-dd91-511c-81fe-eca19c21cfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer/id #uuid "f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb0f50fc-3f9e-56b0-874f-3d40ee14ed33" :answer/id #uuid "bb0f50fc-3f9e-56b0-874f-3d40ee14ed33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2-x+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" :answer/id #uuid "df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2" :answer/id #uuid "3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\pi -1-x-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125255 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,-2,-1)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,-2,-1)+∇f(2,-2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,-2,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.02,$ $y=-1.98,$ and $z=-1.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05) & ≈4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2.02-2,-1.98+2,-1.05+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =4+(0.04-0.04+0.2) \\\\  & =4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124130 :answer-field/correct 17592186124131 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-55146915-2d47-5b59-9386-37303589ba19" :question/id #uuid "55146915-2d47-5b59-9386-37303589ba19" :question/math-academy-id "q-129741" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,0)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =2x{e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,0)=-2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,0) & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1-2(x+1)+y \\\\  & ≈-1-2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71f2f20d-59aa-5f4b-b243-cfacb310155a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71f2f20d-59aa-5f4b-b243-cfacb310155a" :answer-field/id #uuid "71f2f20d-59aa-5f4b-b243-cfacb310155a" :answer-field/key "sel